-- title:
--   Endomorphism of a truncated polynomial ring onto mod t² is bijective
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be a field and let $N, M$ be natural numbers. Write $R = k[X_j : j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,N]$ for the polynomial ring in $N$ variables, let $\mathfrak m = \ker(\mathrm{constantCoeff} : R \to k)$ be the ideal of polynomials with zero constant term, and let $T = R/\mathfrak m^{M+1}$ be the corresponding truncated polynomial ring, with $\mathfrak t$ the image of $\mathfrak m$ in $T$ under the quotient map. Let $\theta : T \to T$ be a $k$-algebra endomorphism of $T$ with the property that for every index $j \in \mathrm{Fin}\,N$ there exists $t \in T$ such that $\theta(t) - \bar X_j$ lies in $\mathfrak t^2$, where $\bar X_j$ denotes the class of $X_j$; that is, each variable lies in the image of $\theta$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal. The conclusion is that $\theta$ is bijective as a map $T \to T$.
--
--   This is the finite-dimensional shadow of the formal inverse function theorem: surjectivity modulo $\mathfrak t^2$ of an endomorphism of the truncated polynomial ring $k[X_1,\dots,X_N]/(X_1,\dots,X_N)^{M+1}$ already forces bijectivity, since $\mathfrak t^{M+1} = 0$ and the ring is a finite-dimensional $k$-vector space. It is used in the comparison of presentations of Artinian local $k$-algebras, in particular by the results on replacing a surjection onto such an algebra by one whose kernel is generated by a prescribed number of elements, and on exhibiting quotients of truncated polynomial rings by a principal ideal as truncated polynomial rings in fewer variables.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPolynomial_bijective_algHom_truncated_of_forall_exists_sub_mem_sq.lean

